ty cost you know we're  scrolling through our Instagram feed and  we feel like that's social but it's not  we just need one on one real live  conversation with another human so it  seems like you're suggesting two things  one is the old you know nothing is but  thinking makes it so  which is just change the way you think  about things the other is change your  actions make these connections with  people and and develop community in a in  an actual way not in the superficial  social media way that's right and this  is this  nomination is kind of changing how you  think and how you act is part of so many  of the different tips another tip we  talk to students about is to take time  for gratitude which is partly a thinking  thing it's like don't focus on the  hassles focus on the blessings in life  and this one is really hard for me you  know when I go out for a glass of wine  with a girlfriend I don't want to talk  about all the wonderful things right  life I want to complain but but it turns  out that that's another spot where our  minds lying to us it's just wrong and  there are some gratitude tricks like the  one you before you go to sleep at night  the three things you're grateful for  that has shown hasn't that shown actual  but more in tests beneficial data on  that yeah that's the amazing thing is  when you do the science on it what you  find are these really simple practices  can change things another activity with  gratitude is what's called a gratitude  letter where you sit down for like you  know 15 minutes and write a letter to  somebody who you're really thankful to  have had in your life but you haven't  had a chance to thank them that act can  boost up well-being ratings a month  later so a simple 15 minute thing that  you can do just thank someone can bump  up your happiness for a whole month  I saw something flash on the screen  random acts of kindness what does that  mean and how does that benefit our own  happiness this is another one where our  minds lied to us you know if I'm having  a bad day I think I'm gonna treat myself  you know I'm gonna go out for a manicure  I'm gonna get a nice mimimi  yes but it turns out that helping others  actually bumps up our happiness more  than we think there's lovely research by  the psychologist Liz done that shows  that the simple act of spending $20 on  someone or spending $5 on someone can  boost up your happiness for a whole day  and can make you happier than spending  that money on yourself what kind of  homework did you assign your students in  this class well we know that it's it's  tough to put these things into practice  so I gave students the homework of doing  these exact practices that I'm  mentioning not for a grade because we  wouldn't grade them at Yale on you know  being nice to someone but just it's in  the syllabus and you have to do it and  so they were simple things like  experience gratitude talk to someone and  other simple healthy habits like sleep  and exercise well professor will we're  very grateful that you have been here  and thank you very much happier having  had this conversation it's already  working thanks so much
 

